{
  "id": "remove-covered-intervals",
  "questionId": "remove-covered-intervals",
  "slug": "remove-covered-intervals",
  "title": "Remove Covered Intervals",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Merge intervals",
  "patterns": [
    "merge intervals",
    "sorting",
    "greedy"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "if you sort intervals by start ascending, and for ties by end descending, can a later interval in that order ever cover an earlier one?",
    "with that sort order, the only way an interval can be covered is if some earlier interval's end reaches at least as far as its own end (since starts are non-decreasing)",
    "sweep through the sorted list keeping track of the maximum end seen among intervals processed so far",
    "an interval is covered exactly when its end is <= the maximum end seen before it (given the sort order handles the start condition automatically); count the intervals that are NOT covered"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "forgetting the end-descending tiebreak when starts are equal, which causes two intervals with the same start to incorrectly both be counted as uncovered",
    "comparing each interval only to the immediately previous one instead of to the maximum end seen so far across all previous intervals",
    "using strict less-than instead of <= when checking if the current end is covered by the running maximum, mishandling duplicate intervals"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given a list of intervals, each represented as [start, end]. An\ninterval is considered covered by another interval if the other interval's\nrange fully contains it (start <= otherStart and end >= otherEnd).\n\n**Remove every interval that is covered by some other interval in the list, and return the number of intervals that remain.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "each interval is [start, end] with start <= end",
      "the input is not guaranteed to be sorted",
      "duplicate intervals may appear"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "duplicate intervals (one covers the other, so only one survives)",
      "a single interval (nothing to cover it)",
      "one interval covers every other interval",
      "no interval covers any other"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "intervals = [[1,4],[3,6],[2,8]]",
        "output": "2",
        "expectedOutput": "2",
        "explanation": "[3,6] is fully covered by [2,8] and gets removed; [1,4] and [2,8] are not covered by anything, so 2 intervals remain"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "intervals = [[1,4],[2,3]]",
        "output": "1",
        "expectedOutput": "1",
        "explanation": "[2,3] is fully covered by [1,4], leaving only [1,4]"
      },
